/* book exam traps: The GCB does not open for every fault. Over-excitation is first corrected by reducing field current; a seized IDG drive is cleared by the drive disconnect, a mechanical action. | 400 Hz from an IDG is constant frequency because a constant-speed drive holds generator speed. A 360-800 Hz variable-frequency supply is not a faulty 400 Hz supply; the range is its design. | Reverse-current cut-out is a DC device that stops the battery driving the generator as a motor. It is not the AC generator circuit breaker. | GCU and GCB are different: the GCU senses, regulates and decides; the GCB carries and breaks generator current. | Using f = p × n ÷ 120 with pole pairs instead of poles. p counts poles: four poles at 12 000 rpm give 400 Hz, not 800 Hz. */
