/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A lead-acid cell has a lead-dioxide positive plate, a sponge-lead negative plate and dilute sulphuric acid electrolyte.
   - Lead-acid gives strong cranking current and is cheap, but heavy per ampere-hour.
   - A nickel-cadmium (NiCd) cell uses nickel hydroxide positive and cadmium negative plates in potassium hydroxide electrolyte.
   - NiCd has low internal resistance, good cold performance and long life.
   - A capacity check discharges the battery at constant current to a stated end voltage (about 1.0 V per NiCd cell), records the ampere-hours removed and compares them with the rated Ah; Worked example 2 does one.
   - Depth of discharge (DoD) = (Ah removed ÷ rated Ah) × 100 %.
   - Lithium-ion cells are about 3.6 to 3.7 V each, so far fewer cells are needed for a 28 V DC system: the B787 main and APU batteries have eight cells in series.
*/
